<h2>The incident and the trial</h2>
<p>On 13 July 2005, following a dispute over a loan, Ganesh Mondal was attacked with bombs. He suffered severe injuries, was hospitalised, and died the following day. The complaint was lodged on 15 July 2005 — two days after the incident. Five accused faced trial under Section 302 read with Section 34 IPC and Sections 3 and 4 of the Explosive Substances Act; one died during the proceedings, two were acquitted, and the two appellants were convicted and sentenced to imprisonment for life. Their appeal, filed in 2016, was decided by the Division Bench on 17 September 2026.</p>

<h2>The four pillars of the affirmance</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Homicidal death established.</strong> The medical evidence supported the prosecution case that the injuries — most probably caused by explosives — led to death, anchoring the ocular account in the post-mortem record.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Identification by five eyewitnesses.</strong> Multiple witnesses consistently placed the two appellants among the assailants who hurled the explosives, establishing identity beyond reasonable doubt.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>FIR delay explained.</strong> Two days were consumed by the victim's urgent hospitalisation and disrupted communications; the delay evidenced crisis, not concoction.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation defects not fatal.</strong> Non-examination of the original investigating officer and other lapses could not displace credible substantive evidence.</div>
</div>

<h2>The doctrine beneath the checklist</h2>
<p>Each pillar reflects a settled appellate principle. FIR delay is a circumstance for explanation, not an automatic discount: courts ask what the informant was doing in the interval, and an interval spent at a hospital bedside answers the question. Eyewitness contradictions are graded: discrepancies on peripheral details are the natural texture of honest testimony, while contradictions on the core — who did what — are what matter. And the defective-investigation argument, a staple of defence submissions, runs into the equally settled response that the criminal justice system does not acquit because the police performed poorly; it acquits when the evidence, viewed as a whole, leaves reasonable doubt. Where five consistent eyewitnesses and supporting medical evidence remain standing, the lapses of the investigating agency do not create that doubt.</p>

<h2>What defence counsel can still take from the judgment</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>The two acquittals at trial show that the same incident yielded different outcomes for different accused — identification evidence is weighed person by person, and the defence succeeds where the core testimony wavers for a particular accused.</p>
<p>Delay arguments should be built on what the interval was actually used for. If the record shows the informant was free to complain and instead consulted and deliberated, delay regains its force.</p>
<p>Investigation defects remain relevant to corroboration: where ocular evidence is weak or partisan, the absence of the investigating officer's testimony can tip the scales — the point failed here because the ocular evidence was strong.</p>
</div>

<h2>The present-day frame</h2>
<p>For offences after 1 July 2024, the corresponding provisions are Section 103 of the BNS for murder and Section 3(5) for common intention, with appeals governed by Chapter XXXI of the BNSS. The evidentiary principles applied here — explanation of delay, grading of contradictions, and the non-fatality of investigative lapses — carry over unchanged under the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam. Appeals of this vintage, decided two decades after the incident, also underline a practical reality of criminal appellate practice: the record made at trial is the record for life, and it is made well or badly in the first instance.</p>
